#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100626 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "cbd0cb3d-cf0f-54db-8cfc-c938e463e4f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5087" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t^2, \\,\\,\\, y=t^2+1, \\quad t \\geq 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx=2t^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t^2=\\dfrac{x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={t}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1.$\n\nFinally, since $t \\geq0$ and $x=2t^2,$ we must have $x \\geq 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+1, \\quad x \\geq 0 .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f3d396ea-caed-51ed-a4d8-0bfc3301f2f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5101" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\csc{t},\\,\\,\\, y=2\\cot{t}, \\quad 0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=x \\\\ y=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{y}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^{2}{t}+\\cos^{2}{t}=1$ by $\\sin^{2}{t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})}^{2} \\\\ 1+{cot}^{2}⁡t & ={csc}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\csc{t} = x$ and $\\cot t =\\dfrac{y}{2}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{(\\frac{y}{2})}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y^2 = 4\\left( x^2-1 \\right).$\n\nFinally, since $0 < t < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\csc t,$ the interval on the $x$-axis is $x \\geq 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf1509d9-45c6-55be-96b2-ecd94d43aab8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302834} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9bdda6-72e2-56ab-ad61-6fb1c887a147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302835} {:answer/id #uuid "19f54f1d-e824-51ed-a708-863820445e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302836} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6b90c3-39cf-5897-ac2d-cb9f6b6d52b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9f7b51-1cf0-51e9-8432-b994b253edbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6b90c3-39cf-5897-ac2d-cb9f6b6d52b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302837} :answer-field/id #uuid "b903b087-ccd3-56b3-8084-f93deb93fe48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b89cfc2a-8e7d-5178-aba5-ef09172b413a" :question/math-academy-id "q-102687" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡t & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡t}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd83642-b933-54a1-bfde-60a80cc4043a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4{x}^{2}-2x+5,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302841} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdc3ea1-71e5-528d-97c0-d0c2e57e8a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}+x+2,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302842} {:answer/id #uuid "6884b9a6-3e6f-5da3-9301-fd9b18aa627f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}+10x-24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302843} {:answer/id #uuid "32a5cbe1-f264-5f8f-8622-a31d47a2d486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302844} {:answer/id #uuid "02f8094e-40bf-52a3-81b8-2f3b4a663eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}-5x-12,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32a5cbe1-f264-5f8f-8622-a31d47a2d486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302844} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36a3d15-0cf4-5e08-87fa-99ae2e72c7af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4b8d660-3a86-5682-af7d-01dd831d3e28" :question/math-academy-id "q-15653" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=5-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5-t\\,⇒\\,t=5-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=5-x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(5-x)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =25-10x+{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}-10x+24.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x=5-t,$ we must have $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71127adc-667e-550b-b248-c0abc924c5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302848} {:answer/id #uuid "639e7aab-4fe3-5266-a8d1-4e35fd7857ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302849} {:answer/id #uuid "833518fc-37c2-57e9-a716-f07028a9e874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302850} {:answer/id #uuid "3b9917f8-b1cf-5686-be81-1af99efb3395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302851} {:answer/id #uuid "d829b1d0-26ac-537a-a3a8-3c05d90cd30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(3+{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b9917f8-b1cf-5686-be81-1af99efb3395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302851} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce80652f-a751-5e0e-90b6-f70e7cd69140" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6f3c3a1-9262-5890-9af4-1c1bf7604cd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-15654" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y=t(9-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{t}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $x=\\sqrt{t},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a19bcd5-75fd-56e9-b93c-1f0f33097874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302855} {:answer/id #uuid "f87de581-41ff-5ae5-87a1-e327c0722145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302856} {:answer/id #uuid "94bb46a3-ebab-5ca7-b8c6-b355e2600d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302857} {:answer/id #uuid "5eafc569-c09a-5b83-b176-cad75ab16314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302858} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a1ba09-26f6-53e7-9178-130dbcd2c0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a1ba09-26f6-53e7-9178-130dbcd2c0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302859} :answer-field/id #uuid "d587d9eb-47a2-5b16-9a07-604038e8c3ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d6247c7-93e8-5757-8717-e162c7d262b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15657" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "567bd9d4-769a-5520-8bf8-0b8331e1f837" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-x},\\,\\,x\\ne 0,\\,1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302862} {:answer/id #uuid "93f17a52-1741-5199-bb8e-083d85ba8c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1+x},\\,\\,x\\ne -1,\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302863} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e8f1e6-b2a9-5cb9-80bc-0eaa3b79907d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1+2x},\\,x\\ne -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302864} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2e3ece-40a8-535e-81a3-05d4eef9a6ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{1-2x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302865} {:answer/id #uuid "ea597a22-e2fb-508b-972c-2ec95d482354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea597a22-e2fb-508b-972c-2ec95d482354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302866} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5db3627-143e-50d1-b347-6643f8b22217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "563cbbce-ad5c-5e6f-8f8d-ab3af5a6315e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,t\\ne -1,\\,2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ into our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{((\\frac{1}{x}-1)-2)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x}-3)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1-3x}{x})} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{1-3x}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -1,\\,2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+1},$ we must have $x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93911837-1abd-5695-838e-0d78fbca8630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098515} {:answer/id #uuid "af696adf-49eb-5094-96ca-2aec3c107333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 4}{5})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 1}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098516} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4674ff-714d-5ade-b3a0-42e6ee63bd4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098517} {:answer/id #uuid "3e74b8c9-fb4a-547e-b007-c516090fccf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x - 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y + 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098518} {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68676ebb-6352-5133-bc22-dd971fb65ce5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{x + 1}{2})^{2} + (\\frac{y - 4}{5})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098519} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb3e85e-b511-5963-8b3f-72d966e1c836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20bf63df-5014-5d31-baeb-e6e95f136242" :question/math-academy-id "q-15751" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{y-4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{y-4}{5})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81bb9a03-c337-5557-bf63-9b6b72dc52f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098521} {:answer/id #uuid "8dafe98c-e0e8-5d1a-8ace-80fcf41a6d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098522} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaaa4e9-cb9d-55d2-88a9-f8a2c63b0d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} = 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098523} {:answer/id #uuid "e7061204-ab95-516a-9a50-4a5fdc5a1efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{2})^{2} - (\\frac{x}{3})^{2} = 2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098524} {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48a824f3-35a5-538a-944c-7fe7e8732431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{y}{3})^{2} + (\\frac{x}{2})^{2} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098525} :answer-field/id #uuid "25da4b65-0173-565c-ba8e-416b6d42051b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0582dce7-ef1a-5cfc-9ee5-447a325b4a34" :question/math-academy-id "q-15752" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${(\\frac{y}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "78dbecb4-68d4-53b0-b47f-f37ce92da896" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically With Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9325d7-f550-5c59-88d7-8ff8a9233ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302870} {:answer/id #uuid "b87282ce-139d-532c-be53-6935258cb3e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-18+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302871} {:answer/id #uuid "b806830b-01c1-5815-b667-6de6e14cfc1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302872} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce38dcf-0d4f-57ad-abc3-15b67f37213c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c7c790-d016-564e-8f2a-a689ca3b0dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302869} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c96509-f5e7-56e3-8067-f632bbdd7285" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6300777-605f-507f-978f-04a8f9538fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-24576" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using double angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=\\frac{x}{6}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=-\\frac{y}{5}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2{(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{5} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18} \\\\ y & =-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-5+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{18}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382} {:answer/id #uuid "b77ea1a3-0a9b-5e4c-9285-4d6023de34ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x-3)(3x+1)(2-3x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2b61af0-015e-5207-9580-411ca515e769" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "((3x-1)^2-4)(2-3x)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285382} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a9e7a06-a7ad-5fea-bd64-541162aae1b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01251fc0-f57a-5dfd-8833-fe87964205c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-321074" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t+1}{3},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-4)(1-t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t+1}{3}\\,⇒\\,t=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=3x-1$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((3x-1)^{2}-4){(1-(3x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x+1-4)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-6x-3)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3{x}^{2}-2x-1)(2-3x)^{2} \\\\  & =3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=3(3x+1)(x-1)(2-3x)^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "79107609-77e6-55e9-970d-da63c39daf0a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af3b780-36c3-519d-8257-c8d055f0cf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32x(1-2x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af3b780-36c3-519d-8257-c8d055f0cf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32x(1-2x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce15f979-8df4-5af1-afec-e04d8ac4a855" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c024990d-5c3f-5250-a9bf-94a1cfb26955" :question/math-academy-id "q-321075" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1-t}{4},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-1)(1+t)^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1-t}{4}\\,⇒\\,t=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1-4x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((1-4x)^{2}-1)((1+(1-4x))^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-8x+16{x}^{2}-1)((2-4x)^{2}) \\\\  & =(-8x+16{x}^{2})(4)(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)(2-4x)^{2} \\\\  & =8x(2x-1)\\cdot 4(1-2x)^{2} \\\\  & =32x(2x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=32x(2x-1)^{3}.$"}]}
